A1. Those who eat chestnuts in the dark. ~ OPTIMISTS
A9. A life saver. ~ SCOT
A13. As big as all out doors. ~ ROOMY
A14. A little article with a definite use. ~ THE
A15. The architecture from here will never be forgotten. ~ IONIA
A17. Something that is always handmade. ~ CLAP
A18. It puts the 1932 cars in the shade. ~ TREE
A19. A constructive idea. ~ BUILD
A20. This could be held for you. ~ HAD
A21. This had a falling out with the head of the concern. ~ HAIR
A22. It has always been averse to sentiment. ~ POEM
A23. He had to use this in order to get a corner on corn. ~ ER
A24. "With a ribbon and a feather and a bit of lace upon it." ~ BONNET
A26. Here is one heavyweight that is not in wrong. ~ TON
A27. We hope this will run at the next presidential election. ~ CAR
A28. On account of the depression. there was a plot to put this in the background. ~ GARDEN
A30. This comes before age but never before beauty. ~ AN
A31. Many fishermen have caught these the last minute after their bait was gone. ~ TRAINS
A34. What the rent was in October. ~ TORN
A35. A memory of Yale. ~ ELI
A36. What a mirror will do to a vain woman. ~ REFLECT
A38. This could either be a part of a split infinite or a split atom. ~ TO
A39. This one would never have been caught, if he had not played hookey from the school. ~ BASS
A40. Who's your humorous Hoosier? (Test your sobriety by repeating this.) ~ ADE
A41. It takes a clipper to do this. ~ TRIM
A43. Popular pretty Polly Psittaci. (Another test for sobriety.) ~ PARROT
A45. Another name for a Lamblike humorist. ~ ELIA
A47. Sprinted. ~ RAN
A48. This will be seeing you soon. ~ ORB
A51. What the young moderns do to embrace their opportunities. ~ PET
A52. The big dummy did this with the cards on the table. ~ LAID
A54. Two vitamins necessary to a cat. ~ CA
A55. It's a great life if you do not. ~ WEAKEN
A57. What a soft shoulder can make any driver do. ~ SKID
A58. What Jack Horner and Miss Muffet did. ~ SAT
A59. An old hen's vocation. ~ LAYER
A60. Who knows? It might have been this instead of the apple that caused all the trouble. ~ PEAR
A61. Make it half and half, using Vert. No. 58. ~ DEMI
A62. Here is a plum for some lucky man. ~ SLOE
A63. What you will need to replace that old car you ditched. ~ PULL
A64. Shakespearean crooner. ~ ROMEO
A65. This manufactures one of the best products of the times. ~ MULTIPLICATION

D1. This bunch of men always work in harmony. ~ ORCHESTRA
D2. Cold and distant. ~ POLAR
D3. A member of the Hopper family. ~ TOAD
D4. What the little pest next door is. ~ IMP
D5. A term of ownership. ~ MY
D6. There are some promoters that would even do this to beans. ~ STRING
D7. Make it at that place. ~ THEREAT
D8. What grandfather is apt to do with his glasses. ~ SEE
D9. Less than half of Siberia. ~ SIB
D10. This always adds interest to the paper. ~ COUPON
D11. A gastronomic delight that has lots of "it" according to Mr. Geo. Rector. ~ ONION
D12. A good thing to slip on after the bath. ~ TILE
D16. Some people think the eclipse, hot weather, sun-spots and moonshine are caused by this. ~ ADMINISTRATION
D18. This covers a multitude of skins. ~ TAN
D21. When this gets mad it flies into a rage. ~ HORNET
D24. The only reason he was not in jail. ~ BAIL
D25. The gaitway to the race track. ~ TROT
D26. This can be increased ten times by adding nothing. ~ TEN
D27. Where an American eats, an Englishman dines, a Dutchman feeds and a Frenchman wines. ~ CAFE
D29. When you do this to a cigarette, it will always light. ~ DROP
D30. "And another thing." ~ ALSO
D32. Sometimes you find traffic heavy but this kind of traffic light. ~ RED
D33. This reflects the brilliancy of the stars. ~ SCREEN
D35. An after vacation thought. ~ EARN
D37. This can make any fat man lean. ~ TILT
D39. If you want to drive the point in, you will have to hit this one on the head. ~ BRAD
D42. This is found in a bunch of keys. ~ MI
D44. A dry spell. ~ ARID
D46. What an Ike this one was. ~ ALKALI
D48. This one got the whoo-ping cough by staying out all night (plur.). ~ OWLS
D49. For this is the kingdom— ~ REALM
D50. An inlet. ~ BAYOU
D51. A byword. ~ PER
D53. What a cockney keeps under 'is 'at. ~ AIR
D54. Relief for the present. ~ CAMEO
D56. It took some hard knocks to make him do this over. ~ KEEL
D57. A good thing to do for a large figure. ~ SELL
D58. The better half. (See Hor. 61.) ~ SEMI
D60. The snappiest little baby at the beauty show. ~ PUP
D61. There is just a small point to this. ~ DOT
D63. What a printer has when he eats his words ~ PI
D64. A vegetarian's favorite dish. ~ RA


